body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Known-Offset Coordinate v1 Snapshot 0.1 [# known-offset-1]

  Status: immutable compatibility snapshot
  [$ aeonic-temporal-known-offset-specs-v1-snapshot-0.1], frozen 2026-11-23.
  Proposal lifecycle and disabled publication are preserved. Existing profiles,
  defaults and frozen artifacts retain their meanings and bytes. Implementations
  MUST satisfy the
  [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.1/known-offset-contract.schema.json | closed snapshot schema]
  and the semantic, admission and diagnostic rules here; schema validity alone
  is insufficient. Matching CTS snapshot:
  [$ aeonic-temporal-known-offset-cts-v1-snapshot-0.1]. Only a complete passing
  genuine execution against that recognized immutable target supports a stable
  implementation claim; artifact checks, fixture replay and native capacity
  qualification establish separate facts. Consumer adoption remains explicit.

  ## 1. Domain, capabilities and selection [# known-offset-2]

  Profile: [$ aeonic.operations.temporal.known-offset.v1]. Complete independent
  capabilities are [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.KnownOffset.Conversion], requiring
  [$ convert], and [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.KnownOffset.Relations], requiring
  [$ equal], [$ compare] and [$ relate]. Every admitted semantic case in a claimed
  capability is required. Host-declared availability is not enabled by a request.
  Relations-only implementations consume externally produced bundles without a
  conversion producer. Neither claim requires another public temporal capability.

  This is ordinary ISO/proleptic Gregorian offset-coordinate arithmetic with
  86,400 coordinate seconds per day. It does not verify physical UTC alignment,
  clock realization, leap authority or exact elapsed SI/UTC time. Missing facts
  are never inferred from the host timezone, locale, current date or database.
  Coordinate shifts/differences, literal materialization, calendar arithmetic,
  offset assignment to unqualified values and timezone/WTC resolution are deferred.

  Require profile in [$ input.policy.profile] or [$ input.context.profile]. Validate
  both supplied configurations, then let policy override context and resolve only
  the effective identifier. Malformed overridden configuration still fails; a
  well-formed overridden unavailable identifier need not resolve. Missing selection
  is [$ invalid_policy] at [$ /input/policy/profile]. An unavailable nonempty scalar
  identifier is [$ unsupported_profile] at its selected field.

  Requests contain exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. Convert input requires
  [$ value] and [$ policy], optionally [$ context]. Relations require [$ left] and
  [$ right], optionally [$ policy] and [$ context]. Context has only optional
  [$ profile]. Convert policy requires [$ destinationOffset] and optionally [$ profile];
  relation policy has only optional [$ profile]. These objects are closed. Context
  cannot supply a destination. Even same-frame/zero conversion names its destination.

  ## 2. Authored admission and deferred claims [# known-offset-3]

  An authored bundle contains required [$ temporal] and optional
  [$ originDateGranularity], [$ originClockForm], [$ history], [$ clockModel],
  [$ timescale]. Its descriptor has exactly [$ category: "knownOffsetDateTime"]
  and scalar-string [$ value]. This category is a nominal admission envelope:
  recognized unknown/unqualified/context-bearing forms are not silently stripped
  or relabeled malformed merely because the category's name includes known offset.

  A dated value has date then [$ T] then clock, optional offset, then optional
  [$ &] context. Date shapes are [$ YYYY-], [$ YYYY-MM], [$ YYYY-MM-DD]. Year is
  0001–9999; validate Gregorian dates, including leap days. Dated clocks are [$ HH],
  [$ HH:], [$ HH:MM], [$ HH:MM:SS] with an optional ASCII decimal fraction on seconds.
  A date-less nominal clock is [$ HH:], [$ HH:MM] or [$ HH:MM:SS] with optional
  fraction and offset, without a leading T or an & context. Both dated hour forms
  have hour origin. Fields have stated widths, hours 00–23, minutes 00–59 and
  nominal seconds 00–60. Fraction has one or more ASCII digits. A context is one
  or more ASCII letters/digits or slash, underscore, hyphen, plus, dot. No whitespace,
  decimal comma, overflow normalization or [$ 24:00] is admitted.

  Offsets are [$ Z] or signed whole-minute [$ HH:MM], with hours 00–23 and minutes
  00–59. [$ -00:00] is the recognized unknown-offset form; it is not known zero.
  No civil-zone whitelist or offset seconds is admitted. These admission rules are
  contract-local and do not change Core syntax/preservation or assert validity in
  an authority-backed UTC model.

  At stage 4 validate the complete nominal text, intrinsic Gregorian/clock ranges
  and supplied metadata. Before any completion expansion, remember whether the
  value is deferred: missing date, missing/unknown offset, second 60, any & context,
  or supplied clockModel/timescale. At stage 5 any such operand returns
  [$ unsupported_domain] at its temporal category path. No completion pieces are
  constructed or charged for a deferred authored form; text/fraction/request guards
  still apply. Validating right input still precedes a deferred left result.
  Malformed nominal text is [$ invalid_operand] at stage 4, not a deferred success.

  If supplied, clockModel/timescale must each be nonempty scalar strings, checked
  at stage 4, then rejected as explicit deferred claims at stage 5. This holds for
  known vocabulary and unknown future declarations alike: this profile assesses
  neither. Missing declarations mean undeclared realization, never verified direct
  UTC. Adapter authors MUST NOT omit supplied extra claims to manufacture eligibility.

  Eligible authored hour/minute forms cover a half-open hour/minute on every
  compatible date; explicit seconds cover one exact coordinate on every compatible
  date, not a measurement bin. Keep all dates of a reduced month/year, never fill
  missing fields or substitute a continuous hull. Preserve authored spelling at
  admission, including fractional trailing zeroes. Fraction digits are exact.

  ## 3. Derived sets and metadata [# known-offset-4]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": {
      "category": "knownOffsetDateTimeSet",
      "offset": "Z",
      "pieces": [{ "kind": "point", "at": "2024-03-01T09:00:00" }]
    },
    "originDateGranularity": "day",
    "originClockForm": "second",
    "history": { "clamping": "none-declared", "approximation": "none-declared" },
    "originalOffset": "+01:00"
  }
  ~~~$

  Derived bundles require every field shown; optional clockModel/timescale use the
  same validate-then-defer rule. Descriptor fields are exactly category, offset,
  pieces. Input offset admits unknown -00:00 structurally, then rejects it at stage
  5; successful bundles always have a known frame. Pieces describe local coordinates
  in that one frame. All ordinary coordinates are canonical full
  [$ YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS] with optional nonzero fraction ending in a nonzero digit.
  Seconds are 00–59. Derived second 60 is malformed canonical coordinate input, unlike
  a nominal authored second-60 admission. Validate actual dates. Trim fractional
  trailing zeroes and omit a zero fraction in produced coordinates; incoming derived
  text must already be canonical. Never round or establish a fixed temporal grid.

  A point has exactly [$ kind: "point"] and [$ at]. An interval has exactly
  [$ kind: "interval"], [$ start], [$ end], [$ includeStart], [$ includeEnd]. Flags
  are Boolean endpoint membership, not predicate outputs. Start is strictly before
  end. Singletons use points; all zero-width intervals are invalid. The sole special
  endpoint is [$ {"boundary":"endOfDomain"}], permitted only as an excluded interval
  end, denoting the start of year 10000 in this set's own frame. It cannot be an
  included end, point, start, infinity or authored literal.

  Pieces are nonempty, ascending, nonoverlapping, without redundant points or
  exactly mergeable neighbors. Compare coordinates exactly by date, clock and
  fraction with virtual zero padding. Adjacent pieces are separate only when their
  touching coordinate is excluded by both. Never sort/repair incoming sets or fill
  a missing point. Validate a piece completely before cross-piece canonicality;
  reject overlap/unsorted/mergeable input at the offending at/start, nonpositive
  width at end, and an included upper marker at includeEnd.

  Origins use year/month/day/undeclared and hour/minute/second/undeclared.
  History is the closed object with clamping none-declared/clamping-declared and
  approximation none-declared/approximation-declared. Authored omitted origins
  are inferred independently from shape; omitted history defaults both markers
  to none-declared. Derived markers are required and never inferred from endpoints.
  Valid explicit origins remain descriptive history even when differing from the
  current shape. No marker selects coverage or implies an uncertainty interval.

  originalOffset is a known offset string or [$ "undeclared"]. For authored input
  initialize from the eligible suffix; authored bundles do not admit a separate
  originalOffset override. For derived input require it explicitly. Undeclared
  source history can coexist with a required known current frame. A historical
  known offset need not match that frame. It does not reinterpret current coverage
  or authenticate provenance. Repeated conversion preserves it unchanged, retaining
  original Z versus +00:00 identity. Detailed source literals/logs remain application
  provenance rather than a required standard operation log.

  Other recognized descriptor categories are civilDate, civilDateSet, civilDateTime,
  civilDateTimeSet, civilClock, civilClockSet, finiteNumber, exactNumber, exactRational,
  string, boolean, symbol, positiveInfinity, negativeInfinity, nan, explicitNull,
  explicitAbsence, toggle, hex, radix, encoding, separator, sansaAddress, referenceForm,
  temporal, container. Validate their containing bundle/supplied origins, history
  and claim strings; their descriptor payload is opaque after a present recognized
  string category. Stage 5 rejects at category. Unknown categories are invalid.
  These deferred bundles allow the authored optional fields but not originalOffset.
  Opaque payloads still pay request admission. No tag can opt out of required cases.

  ## 4. Translation, bounds and relations [# known-offset-5]

  For reasoning let x be exact local ordinary seconds since 0001-01-01T00:00:00 and
  o the signed offset in seconds. The common coordinate position is x minus o.
  This is a mathematical chart, not a claim about physical clock realization.
  Conversion from source o to destination d translates every coordinate by d minus
  o. Preserve flags, gaps, order and piece count. No day/second/interior enumeration
  or midnight splitting is required. Internal representations are implementation
  choices; host binary rounding or fixed precision changes are forbidden.

  Let B = 315537897600 be the excluded upper local boundary. All represented
  coverage must remain inside the local domain from zero inclusive to B exclusive.
  A translated end may equal B only when excluded, and must emit the marker there.
  Translate the source marker's actual coordinate; when it lands inside the target
  domain, emit an ordinary endpoint. Beyond-boundary exclusions still imply
  out-of-domain coverage. Reject the whole conversion with unrepresentable_result;
  never clip, widen or emit partial/empty coverage. For a nonempty positive-width
  piece, lower bound zero is legal with either inclusion flag.

  Relations compare exact common-position sets, admitting wider internal positions
  without imposing a hypothetical stored zero-frame domain check. Frame spelling,
  origins, histories and originalOffset do not affect covered-set equality.

  - equal returns symbols equal or notEqual for exact equality.
  - compare returns equal for identical sets, before only if every left member is
    strictly earlier than every right member, after for the converse, otherwise
    indeterminate. A half-open minute is before its excluded ending point. Disjoint
    interleaved sets may compare indeterminately; this is not a total sorting order.
  - relate returns exactly equal, strict contains, strict containedBy, overlaps
    for a shared member without containment, or disjoint.

  For example 10:00:00+01:00 and 09:00:00Z on the same full date compare equal.
  0001-01-01T00:30:00+01:00 is before 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z, but converting the first
  to Z fails bounds. The final year-9999 minute at +02:00 converted to +01:00 has
  ordinary upper endpoint 9999-12-31T23:00:00, not an unchanged source marker.

  ## 5. Closed successes and diagnostics [# known-offset-6]

  Success has exactly outcome value, value and metadata. Conversion value is a
  complete derived bundle; pass that value, not the response envelope, to another
  operation. Preserve both origins, both sticky histories and originalOffset.
  Conversion changes only local coordinates and the current frame. Reversing or
  zero conversion never clears histories. No new clamping/approximation is applied.

  Metadata has evaluation exact-for-policy, clamping not-applied and effectivePolicy.
  For conversion effectivePolicy has exactly resolved profile and destinationOffset;
  for relations it has exactly profile. Relations value is the existing closed
  symbol descriptor with category symbol and the unwrapped symbolic name as value.
  Its metadata additionally requires operandHistory, operandOrigin and
  operandOriginalOffset, each with exactly left and right. Origins contain exactly
  dateGranularity and clockForm. Exact current evaluation makes no source accuracy,
  UTC realization, provenance authentication or uncertainty claim.

  Diagnostic has exactly outcome diagnostic, reason and context, optionally scalar
  message. Context has exactly integer stage 1–7 and path, a JSON Pointer or null.
  A diagnostic has no value or successful metadata. Reasons are invalid_operand,
  unsupported_operation, invalid_policy, unsupported_profile, unsupported_domain,
  unrepresentable_result, resource_limit. Empty/noncanonical/malformed operands are
  invalid_operand; recognized deferred operands are unsupported_domain. Invalid or
  missing destination is invalid_policy, including -00:00. No failure automatically
  produces a Boolean, symbol, null or empty temporal set.

  ## 6. Ordered validation [# known-offset-7]

  Stop at the first failing substep in the following order.

  1. Scalar text, actual logical JSON UTF-8 bytes and per-path container depth before
     deeper decoding: invalid_operand or reached resource_limit, null path.
  2. Strict JSON/duplicate keys; closed envelope/input structure, operation
     availability, operation-specific arity: invalid_operand or unsupported_operation.
     Duplicate keys are invalid_operand at stage 2 with null path.
  3. Context object, policy object/required destination, context profile, policy
     profile, destination value, then effective profile selection/availability:
     invalid_policy or unsupported_profile. A missing destination wins over malformed
     supplied profiles; a malformed destination wins over unavailable profile.
  4. Conversion value, or relation left then right. Validate category, bundle
     metadata/closure, descriptor closure, text/precision, canonicality and aggregate
     expansion allowance at the points below: invalid_operand or resource_limit.
  5. Deferred domain rejection in operand order, unsupported_domain at category.
  6. Full work count, conversion candidate count, then conversion result bounds:
     resource_limit before unrepresentable_result. Relations have no candidates or
     stored result-domain test. Operation failures have null path.
  7. Final output piece count, then successful compact result bytes: resource_limit
     with null path. Required diagnostics are never recursively suppressed by the
     successful-output byte limit.

  Within a closed object check required fields in their schema order, then unknown
  keys in decoded Unicode-scalar order. JSON Pointer escapes are standard. At stage
  2 require operation then input, reject unknown request fields, require input object,
  reject unknown generic input fields (left/right/value/context/policy vocabulary),
  check operation availability, then required arity and unexpected known fields.
  Relations require left then right. Conversion requires value; policy's required
  presence/destination is a stage-3 policy check rather than a stage-2 arity failure.

  At stage 4 require temporal object and string category first. Supported derived
  bundles then require date origin, clock origin, history, originalOffset, reject
  unknown bundle fields, validate history (clamping then approximation), date origin,
  clock origin, originalOffset, clockModel, timescale. Authored/deferred supplied
  metadata use the same order, with absent authored defaults applied after valid
  nominal parsing. Descriptor required/unknown fields precede payload. A set checks
  offset before pieces. Empty arrays fail before length capacity. Charge full array
  length against the remaining aggregate input allowance before visiting contents.
  Each piece checks required/unknown fields, at/start, end, flags, boundary placement,
  width and cross-piece canonicality, in that order.

  A literal/ordinary coordinate's decoded character length is guarded before parse;
  fraction length, including authored trailing zeroes, before fractional conversion
  or derived trailing-zero canonicality. Nominal intrinsic validity precedes expansion
  count. Only otherwise eligible authored forms consume their completion-piece count;
  check this against the remaining request-wide allowance before allocation. Derived
  arrays consume their supplied length even if a later eligibility check defers them.
  Earlier stage-4 failures in left input precede all right validation. Within a later
  operand, a reached remaining-capacity failure precedes an unvisited payload error.

  Input piece failures use the reached operand's temporal/pieces path for a derived
  array, temporal for authored expansion. Text/fraction/lexical errors use value or
  the coordinate field. Derived input offset errors use offset. Nominal deferred
  forms and supplied extra claims use category at stage 5. History/origin/claim
  shape errors name the reached field. Operation budgets, bounds and final output
  failures use null paths. These are semantic contract pointers, not outer CTS paths.

  ## 7. Portable resources and harness boundary [# known-offset-8]

  Conversion charges one work unit and one intermediate candidate per expanded
  input piece P. Relations charge L + R work units. Neither enumerates an interval's
  interior dates or coordinates nor constructs a Cartesian product. Optimizations,
  early relation answers and same-frame conversion retain the complete portable
  charge. Successful conversion preserves P canonical pieces. Expanded input,
  intermediate candidates and canonical output are independently limited.

  The explicit CTS baseline is maxRequestBytes 65536, maxRequestDepth 32,
  maxTemporalCharacters 1024, maxInputPieces 1024, maxIntermediatePieces 1024,
  maxOutputPieces 1024, maxWork 2048, maxFractionDigits 256, maxResultBytes 262144.
  These are test settings, not measured native defaults. maxInputPieces is a total
  across operands, unlike the existing civil date-time per-operand allowance; existing
  contracts/snapshots are unchanged. Two leap-year literals can fit together.

  Character limits count decoded Unicode scalars; byte limits count logical UTF-8.
  For structured logical requests, maxRequestBytes measures compact JSON with no
  insignificant whitespace and minimal string escaping. Surround each key/string
  with quotes; escape quotation mark and reverse solidus; use the two-character
  JSON escapes for backspace, form feed, newline, carriage return and tab. Encode
  other U+0000–U+001F controls as six ASCII characters using a lowercase hexadecimal
  Unicode escape. Emit every other Unicode scalar as its literal UTF-8 bytes,
  including non-ASCII, non-BMP, U+2028 and U+2029; do not ASCII-escape these or escape
  solidus. Reject unpaired surrogates rather than replacing them. Use one colon
  between each key/value and one comma between members/elements; container braces,
  brackets and literal tokens contribute their actual bytes. Key order does not
  affect byte count. Preserve supplied raw numerical token spellings in structured
  CTS requests, including -0 and large exponents; a native API documents its mapping
  of host numbers to logical token values before this measurement and cannot recover
  source spellings already lost by a caller.

  Raw requestText instead pays its supplied UTF-8 bytes verbatim, including
  whitespace, escape spellings and numerical tokens. Never parse and compact it
  before measuring. For both forms, container depth counts the logical root object
  or array as one, each directly nested object/array adds one, and scalar children
  add nothing. Do not count outer CTS envelopes, raw-string wrapping or implementation
  token wrappers as logical containers. Guard bytes and depth incrementally before
  deep decoding/materialization; a deep raw fixture must reach its stage-1 limit
  diagnostic without relying on the host JSON decoder's recursion capacity.

  Fractions count supplied ASCII digits including trailing zeroes. Input arrays
  and eligible literal expansions pay the full count. Result bytes use the same
  minimal escaping and compact encoding for the entire successful logical envelope,
  including all metadata. Piece maxima do not guarantee every precision/metadata
  combination fits the byte limit. Use bounded/saturating arithmetic before
  allocations. Structured and raw representations of the same logical value may
  therefore have different byte counts, but each has one defined counting recipe.

  All limits are per operation; applications manage chain budgets. Trusted limits
  are outside logical policy/context and must be an object of known nonnegative
  safe integers. Explicit null, arrays, booleans, fractional/unsafe values and
  unknown names are harness errors, not defaults or operation diagnostics. Omitted
  known fields may use documented native defaults; CTS supplies complete maps.

  Raw JSON admission preserves numeric token spelling and duplicate-key evidence
  inside opaque payloads, including negative zero and oversized exponents. Native
  structured admission handles cycles/depth/shared graphs in bounded work without
  exponentially re-expanding shared subtrees. Document host-specific unsupported
  getters/proxies/subclasses as host boundaries, not semantic opt-outs. Unknown
  logical fields never raise trusted capacities. No timeout or private optimization
  changes the portable admission recipe.

  ## 8. Conformance boundaries [# known-offset-9]

  CTS MUST check each complete capability independently, all four operations
  for combined claims, exact cross-offset coverage, flags/gaps, reduced-date
  completion, fractions, frame/zero identity and metadata. Combined and conversion
  claims exercise actual returned-value chains; expected bundles may validate
  fixtures but cannot substitute for the SUT's returned bundle at execution time.
  Relations verify symmetry/inversion and strict-before transitivity without a
  sorting API. Law inputs MUST include a distinct-input equality pair and at least
  one strict-before transitivity witness; passing zero assertions for a required
  law is not coverage. Conversion claims require a successful linked conversion-to-
  conversion chain. Relations claims require a non-vacuous relation law group;
  combined claims additionally require a successful linked conversion-to-relation
  chain. Reject a target lacking the selected capability's required
  coverage before reporting artifact validity or a complete execution pass.
  Compound failures verify the exact stages/paths above.

  Artifact/schema validation is not implementation execution or stable conformance.
  Capacity probes are separate from mandatory semantic cases. Mutable next 0.2
  remains separate and cannot establish this snapshot's stable claim. Snapshot
  capture preserves semantics, fixtures and allowances; it does not promote the
  specification lifecycle. No physical UTC/authority resolution, package release
  or other implementation claim follows from this capture.
`
